  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 137 AM EDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nAs the cold sector of a low over northern Ontario rolls through the \narea this morning, local observations are showing winds veer to the \nwest and northwest with time, with shots of CAA arriving today and \nagain tonight into Thursday morning. These shots of CAA will bring \nlight lake effect rain showers back over the northwest wind belts, \nmainly over the east. Expect the rainfall to be rather light, no \nmore than a few hundreths of an inch. While some lightning could be \nseen in some of the rain showers today into Thursday, expect it to \nbe rather rare due to the limited instability really only found in \nthe lowest levels of the atmosphere. While the strong lapse rates \nnear the sfc will keep high temperatures in the 60s to potentially \neven lower 70s across the area today, the cooler air from the train \nof CAA brings more normal Fall-like temperatures to the sfc Thursday \nand Friday as the daytime highs only reach the mid 50s to mid 60s on \nThursday and the 60s on Friday as some WAA is seen during the latter \nhalf of the day. Otherwise, expect lows generally in the 30s to \npotentially upper 20s tonight and Thursday night across the interior \nareas. Outside of the rain and cooler temperatures for the latter \nportion of the work week, expect breezy west to northwest winds of \n20 to 30 mph today and Thursday across the U.P., highest along the \nLake Superior shoreline.\n\nMoving into the weekend time frame a low pressure lifting off of the \nRockies in Alberta splits into two separate lows, one moving east \nalong the Canadian Prairies/Northern Plains and another that \ncontinues sliding southwards near the Front Range, phasing with \nanother low from the Pacific in the Desert Southwest early next \nweek. While we should be setup under warm air from the Southern \nPlains, with a tropical disturbance moving into the Southeastern U.S \nthis weekend that lifts into the Lower Great Lakes early next week, \nexpect mainly dry weather to continue over the U.P. and Upper Great \nLakes this weekend into potentially early next week. Thus, while the \nEuropean and NBM ensembles are showing a strong signal for well \nabove normal temperatures (highs in the 70s and lows in the 40s and \n50s), we should remain precipitation-free until early next week at \nthe earliest. While there are some questions on how dry the airmass \nwill be this weekend, there may enough moisture within said airmass \nadvecting in to prevent elevated fire weather conditions as some \ngusty southerly winds can be expected via a robust LLJ (potentially \n50+ knots). Rainfall may return early next week as wrap-around \nmoisture could move back in from the north via CAA from Canada.",
